Undifferentiated Tremor
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Action or postural bilateral tremor | Complete absence of Parkinsonian features (no rigidity/bradykinesia) | No identifiable metabolic or toxicologic etiology

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-045
Reason for Visit: Acute nocturnal distress due to severe shaking of the hands, requiring urgent evaluation to exclude an acute stroke, alcohol withdrawal, or severe thyroid storm.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute-onset, uncontrollable limb tremors. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out acute central neurological pathology, acute toxicity, or hypermetabolic crisis, preventing severe systemic deterioration prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My hands will not stop shaking, I cannot even hold a glass of water, and I am terrified I am getting Parkinson's disease.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a mild to moderate subtle shaking of the hands and limbs that acutely worsened tonight. The patient reports a distinct exacerbation with anxiety, fatigue, and excessive caffeine intake earlier in the day. The tremor is predominantly postural or action-induced rather than resting. The patient explicitly denies any progressive neurological decline, muscle stiffness, or slowness of movement prior to this flare.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently unable to perform fine motor tasks safely.
Social/Safety Concerns: High somatic anxiety; potential for burns or spills with hot liquids.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/80 mmHg | HR: 86 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Patient appears highly anxious, visibly holding their hands tightly to suppress the shaking. An incidental chart review notes a history of a skipped beat sensation with an asymptomatic presentation and documented low-burden ectopy, which is stable and unrelated to the acute neurological complaint.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination reveals a high-frequency and low-amplitude movement in the outstretched hands (postural tremor) that worsens with finger-to-nose testing (kinetic tremor) but resolves completely when the hands are completely supported and at rest in the lap. There is an absolute absence of significant bradykinesia or cogwheel rigidity. The patient maintains a normal gait and balance. Handwriting is tremulous but intact, with minimal micrographia. Thyroid palpation is normal and no exophthalmos is present.
Environmental Observations: Two large, empty energy drink cans are visible in the recycling bin.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking no tremor-inducing medications (e.g., lithium, valproate).

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Undifferentiated Tremor - Status: Acute physiological exacerbation of an essential tremor, triggered by sympathomimetic overload.
Differentials:

Parkinson's Disease - Ruled out definitively due to the complete absence of a true resting tremor (pill-rolling), lack of bradykinesia, and normal, fluid gait.

Thyrotoxicosis - Ruled out clinically due to the absence of severe resting tachycardia, weight loss, and normothermia.

Acute Alcohol Withdrawal (Delirium Tremens) - Ruled out due to the patient's clear sensorium, lack of profound diaphoresis/tachycardia, and denial of chronic heavy alcohol use.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low.

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The sudden inability to control fine motor movements mandated an immediate after-hours evaluation to exclude a life-threatening neurological or metabolic crisis. Telephone triage correctly identified the high risk of acute central pathology or toxicity. A comprehensive neurological examination definitively localized the issue to a benign, action-induced tremor heavily exacerbated by massive caffeine intake and anxiety, explicitly ruling out the hallmark resting rigidity of Parkinson's disease or the systemic collapse of a hypermetabolic state. This hands-on reassurance immediately terminated the patient's panic cycle and successfully avoided a highly disruptive and unindicated emergency department admission.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided and strict trigger avoidance implemented. The expected investigation results include normal structural brain imaging and normal thyroid function tests, which will assist in confirming the diagnosis of a benign essential tremor via outpatient neurology review.
Positive Behavioral Change: To actively suppress the tremor, consume a large glass of cold water and immediately sit in a quiet, dark room performing progressive muscle relaxation for 20 minutes; actively lowering your sympathetic nervous system drive will flush the caffeine response and stop the muscular misfiring.
Home-Based Services: None indicated tonight.
DME/Safety: Use two hands and a lid when consuming hot liquids until the tremor subsides.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 7 days for baseline thyroid function tests and potential initiation of low-dose beta-blockers for tremor control.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the tremor spreads to the face or head, if sudden weakness develops on one side of the body, or if the patient becomes confused or begins hallucinating.

PATIENT INFORMATION

